{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** Normal heart valves make a sound only when they close. The first heart sound (S1, "lub") is closure of the mitral and tricuspid valves at the start of ventricular systole. The second (S2, "dub") is closure of the aortic and pulmonary valves at the end of systole. S3 and S4 are low-pitched diastolic sounds, usually inaudible in healthy adults.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="" phase="Phase 1" /%}

## The basics

When you listen to a normal heart you hear "lub, dub, lub, dub".

{% figure src="/learn-images/physiology/heart-sounds/hs-wiggers-phonocardiogram.webp" alt="Wiggers diagram with heart sounds aligned to ECG and pressures." caption="S1 and S2 mark the start of systole and diastole." credit="DanielChangMD revised original work of DestinyQx; Redrawn as SVG by xavax, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 2.5" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wiggers_Diagram.svg" width="960" height="735" /%}

- The **"lub"** is the **first heart sound (S1)**. It comes from closure of the atrioventricular (AV) valves, the mitral and tricuspid, at the onset of ventricular systole.
- The **"dub"** is the **second heart sound (S2)**. It comes from closure of the semilunar valves, the aortic and pulmonary, at the end of systole.

Normal heart valves make a sound **only when they close**. The AV valves open silently in early diastole, when ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure. Normally only two heart sounds are audible at the bedside, although four can be recorded on a phonocardiogram.

Ventricular systole is the interval between S1 and S2. As a practical aid, always feel the carotid pulse while you listen. S1 coincides with the carotid upstroke, and S2 comes after it.

## How are heart sounds produced?

It was once thought that the sounds came from the valve leaflets themselves. That view is not fully tenable, because normal valve tissue is not rigid or thick enough to make a sound just by leaflets meeting. Instead:

- The sound comes from the **tensing of the closed valves** as pressure gradients rise rapidly. This sets the whole system into vibration: the valves, the heart muscle, the blood mass and the walls of the great vessels.
- Sudden deceleration of blood produces heart sounds.
- In general, all cardiac sound, normal or abnormal, results from abrupt accelerating or decelerating forces that stretch the surrounding cardiac structures to their elastic limits. The more rapid the forces, the louder the sound and the higher its frequency.
- Low-frequency sounds (S3 and S4) reflect slower events in filling.

## Pitch, duration and what the ear can hear

- Each of the heart sounds lasts slightly more than 0.10 second. S1 lasts about 0.14 second and S2 about 0.11 second. S2 is shorter because the semilunar valves are tauter than the AV valves, so they vibrate for less time.
- The audible heart sounds begin at about 40 cycles per second, the lowest frequency the ear can detect, and go above 500 cycles per second.
- Much of the heart sound energy lies below the audible range, so these parts can be recorded electronically by phonocardiography even though a stethoscope cannot pick them up.
- **S2 has a higher frequency than S1.** There are two reasons: the semilunar valves are tauter than the AV valves, and the taut arterial walls that act as the vibrating chambers for S2 are more elastic than the looser ventricles that vibrate for S1.
- Human hearing covers 20 to 20,000 Hz and is most sensitive between 1,000 and 5,000 Hz. The ear needs a gap of at least 20 ms to tell two sounds apart. Because the ear is less sensitive at low frequencies, low-frequency sounds are the ones most easily missed.

{% table %}
- **Feature**
- **S1 ("lub")**
- **S2 ("dub")**
---
- **Cause**
- Closure of mitral and tricuspid valves
- Closure of aortic and pulmonary valves
---
- **Timing**
- Start of ventricular systole; coincides with the carotid upstroke
- End of systole; comes after the carotid upstroke
---
- **Duration**
- About 0.14 second
- About 0.11 second
---
- **Pitch**
- Lower
- Higher
---
- **Best heard at**
- Mitral area (apex)
- Aortic and pulmonary areas
---
- **Normal splitting**
- Single on auscultation
