{% /table %}

## The first heart sound (S1)

S1 has two components, mitral (M1) and tricuspid (T1). The mitral valve closes just before the tricuspid valve. The two components are very low pitched and merge, so the split of S1 cannot normally be detected by auscultation. If splitting of S1 is clearly heard, it is considered pathological.

S1 is best heard at the **apex (mitral area)**. It is called **soft** when it is softer than S2 at the apex, and **loud** when it is heard with the same intensity at the base (aortic and pulmonary areas) as at the mitral area.

### What decides the loudness of S1?

- **Position of the mitral leaflets at the end of diastole and their closing velocity.** This matters more than contractility. If the leaflets are wide open deep in the left ventricle at the start of systole, S1 is loud. If they are already nearly closed, S1 is soft.
- **PR interval (inversely related).** S1 is loudest at a short PR interval. A long PR interval gives a softer S1, and a very long one can make S1 faint or absent because the mitral valve has already closed before ventricular pressure develops.
- **Left ventricular contractility (directly related).** A more vigorous contraction gives a louder S1. Hyperadrenergic states such as excitement, fever and exercise raise its amplitude.
- **Loss of isovolumetric contraction** leads to a soft S1 (mitral regurgitation, aortic regurgitation, ventricular septal defect).
- **Distance and tissue between the heart and stethoscope.** Anything that increases the distance reduces the sound.

{% table %}
- **Loud S1**
- **Soft or muffled S1**
- **Variable S1**
---
- Mitral stenosis (early, mild to moderate), tricuspid stenosis
- Mitral and tricuspid regurgitation (valves wide apart and do not meet)
- Atrial fibrillation
---
- Tachycardia
- Late mitral stenosis with calcified, rigid leaflets
- Ventricular tachycardia (AV dissociation)
---
- Short PR interval
- Long PR interval (first degree heart block), bradycardia
- Complete heart block (cannon sound)
---
- Hyperkinetic circulation (anaemia, fever, exercise, pregnancy, thyrotoxicosis)
- Aortic regurgitation
- Multiple extrasystoles
---
- Thin people, children
- Obesity, emphysema, thick chest wall, pericardial effusion
-
{% /table %}

Other causes of a soft S1 include shock, acute myocardial infarction, constrictive pericarditis, advanced cardiomyopathy, and exposure to beta-blockers.

**Splitting of S1.** Wide splitting is seen in complete right bundle branch block (RBBB), left ventricular pacing and Ebstein's anomaly. Reversed splitting, where M1 comes after T1, is seen in complete left bundle branch block (LBBB), right ventricular pacing, severe mitral stenosis and left atrial myxoma.

## The second heart sound (S2)

S2 has two components: **A2** (aortic valve closure) and **P2** (pulmonary valve closure). It happens because of sudden deceleration of blood in the aorta and pulmonary artery. A2 matches the incisura of the aortic pressure pulse, and P2 matches the incisura of the pulmonary pressure pulse. Both occur at the end of the T wave on the ECG.

S2 is best heard in the aortic and pulmonary areas. It is a high-frequency sound.

### Normal (physiological) splitting

- The aortic valve closes **before** the pulmonary valve, so S2 is normally split.
- The split is **widest in inspiration** and narrow in expiration. In expiration S2 may sound single.
- The split is best heard in the pulmonary area.

**Why inspiration widens the split.** During inspiration, the capacitance of the pulmonary vascular bed increases. The rise of pulmonary arterial pressure is delayed, which prolongs the pulmonary "hang-out" interval. P2 is therefore delayed (this contributes about 73% of the widening) and A2 comes slightly earlier (about 27%).

**Standing.** Physiological splitting is present in inspiration and **disappears on standing** because venous return falls. A split that **persists** on standing is a pathological split and an important clue to heart disease.

Practical point: S2 is heard as a single sound when A2 and P2 are 20 ms or less apart. Audible expiratory splitting therefore means an interval of at least 30 to 40 ms.

### Abnormal splitting

{% table %}
- **Type**
- **What you hear**
- **Examples**
---
- **Wide and variable**
- Wide split that still varies with respiration
- RBBB (most common cause of expiratory splitting from delayed P2); severe mitral regurgitation
---
- **Wide and fixed**
- Wide split that does not change with respiration
- Atrial septal defect (ASD)
---
- **Reversed (paradoxical)**
- Split audible in expiration and not in inspiration, the opposite of normal
- LBBB, severe aortic stenosis, severe left ventricular dysfunction
---
- **Single S2**
- Only one component heard
- Tetralogy of Fallot, pulmonary stenosis (soft P2)
---
- **Narrow split**
- A2 and P2 close together
- Pulmonary hypertension
{% /table %}

If a split does not change with respiration at all, it is called **fixed**.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Split in one line:** normal split opens on inspiration and closes on expiration. Fixed split is ASD. Wide and variable is RBBB. Reversed split is LBBB or severe aortic stenosis.
{% /callout %}

### Loud and soft A2 and P2

- **Loud A2:** systemic hypertension, aortic aneurysm, aortitis (syphilitic aortitis gives a "tambour" A2), hyperkinetic circulatory states. A loud A2 is also noted with a bicuspid aortic valve.
- **Soft A2:** aortic stenosis, aortic regurgitation from valvular disease.
- **Loud P2:** pulmonary hypertension. A palpable P2 (diastolic shock) denotes pulmonary hypertension.
- **Soft P2:** pulmonary stenosis, tetralogy of Fallot, thick chest wall, COPD, obesity.

Inspiration often reduces the sound of P2 because lung tissue lies between the pulmonary valve and the chest wall.

## The third heart sound (S3)

S3 occurs during the **rapid filling phase of ventricular diastole**. Most of the ventricular end-diastolic volume, roughly 70% to 80%, is achieved in early and mid-diastole, well before atrial contraction. S3 is explained as the inrushing blood setting the ventricle into vibration, with stretching of the papillary muscles and chordae at the time of maximum filling.

- It is **low pitched**, and best heard with the **bell**.
- **Left-sided S3** is best heard at the apex, with the patient in the left lateral position, during expiration.
- **Right-sided S3** is best heard at the lower left sternal border (or subxiphoid area), during inspiration.
- It occurs about 0.16 second after the aortic component of S2 when heard normally in children.

**When is S3 normal?** In children, adolescents and young adults, and also in athletes and pregnancy. In older adults it indicates **systolic heart failure** and carries important prognostic weight.

**Causes of a pathological S3:**

- **Left ventricular S3:** left ventricular failure, aortic regurgitation, mitral regurgitation, ischaemic heart disease, cardiomyopathy
- **Right ventricular S3:** right ventricular failure, endomyocardial fibrosis
- Other: tricuspid regurgitation, myocardial infarction, ASD, VSD, PDA, and high-output states

## The fourth heart sound (S4)

S4 is produced during **atrial systole**, when the atria contract and blood is pushed into the ventricle. It happens when the mitral or tricuspid cusps are tensed by the atrial contraction. It is very weak and low in frequency (about 20 cycles per second or less), and is normally inaudible.

An audible S4 means the atrium is contracting forcefully against a stiff or non-compliant ventricle.

- **Absent in atrial fibrillation**, since there is no atrial contraction.
- **Ischaemic heart disease:** an audible S4 is a hallmark. In acute myocardial infarction an S4 is present in 80 to 90% of patients, though it may take a day or two to appear.
- **Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy:** S4 is characteristic.
- **Acute mitral regurgitation:** the atrium forcefully contracts into a normal-sized ventricle and gives an S4. In chronic mitral regurgitation an S4 is usually absent, but an S3 is common. An S4 in a patient with mitral regurgitation strongly suggests recent onset.
- **Severe aortic stenosis, pulmonary or systemic hypertension, elderly patients**, and hyperkinetic states such as anaemia or thyrotoxicosis.
- A long PR interval (first degree AV block) may allow an audible S4 even without other disease.

## Gallops

- **Triple rhythm:** three audible sounds, S1, S2 and S3 or S4.
- **Quadruple rhythm:** four audible sounds, S1, S2, S3 and S4.
- **Summation gallop:** S3 and S4 merge because of tachycardia.

## Other diastolic sounds

- **Opening snap (OS):** a high-pitched sound of mitral stenosis, heard a short distance after S2. It arises when the stenotic but mobile anterior mitral leaflet suddenly stops moving and domes into the left ventricle as the valve opens. The interval from A2 to OS is **inversely proportional** to the height of the pressure gradient between the left atrium and left ventricle. An opening snap is audible only if the anterior mitral leaflet is mobile, and S1 and OS both become softer as the leaflet calcifies and stiffens.
- **Pericardial knock:** a high-pitched, early diastolic sound of **constrictive pericarditis**. It marks the sudden stop of ventricular filling. It comes earlier than S3 and has a higher frequency. A palpable form is called a diastolic knock.
- **Tumour plop:** a rare, low-pitched sound of atrial myxoma, caused by diastolic prolapse of the tumour across the mitral valve.
- **Ejection and nonejection clicks:** a nonejection click is related to mitral valve prolapse. Squatting increases preload and afterload and moves the click and murmur away from S1. Standing moves them closer to S1.

## Auscultation technique

**Areas.** The heart is auscultated in four areas: the **mitral, tricuspid, pulmonary and aortic areas**. S1 is best heard in the mitral area, and S2 in the aortic and pulmonary areas.

**Stethoscope.**

- The **bell** is used for **low-frequency sounds** (about 80 to 150 Hz): S3, S4 and the mid-diastolic murmur of mitral stenosis.
- The **diaphragm** is used for **high-frequency sounds** (above 300 Hz): S1, S2, opening snap, clicks, systolic murmurs and early diastolic murmurs.

**Order of examination.** Assess the arterial and venous pulses and examine the precordium before auscultation. Develop a routine so you do not miss subtle findings. Identify S1 and S2, then extra sounds (S3 and S4), then clicks and snaps, then murmurs (timing, duration, character, pitch, intensity, location and radiation), then pericardial rubs.

**Making gallops easier to hear.** Raise both legs to 60 degrees for 30 seconds, which increases cardiac output.

**Why sounds can be hard to hear:**

- Anything between the stethoscope and the heart reduces the sound: fat, breast tissue, muscle, or lung with increased air (emphysema). Lean people tend to have louder sounds.
- Environmental noise in clinics and hospital rooms is far higher than in an ideal quiet room, and it can hide high-frequency sounds and murmurs.
- Low-frequency sounds can mask higher-frequency events. High frequencies do not usually mask lower ones.

## Murmurs in brief

**Murmurs** are abnormal sounds caused by vibration of the valves, the heart wall or the great vessels. They are systolic (between S1 and S2), diastolic (between S2 and S1) or continuous. Murmurs are produced by turbulent flow:

- Blood flowing through an **abnormal orifice**, for example in mitral stenosis or regurgitation
- **High-velocity flow through a normal orifice**, for example in anaemia or hyperkinetic states

**Innocent murmurs** are soft systolic murmurs in people with no cardiac abnormality. They are more common in children (Still's murmur), usually heard in the pulmonary area, and can occur when stroke volume is increased, as in pregnancy and in athletes.

**Effect of respiration.** Right-sided sounds and murmurs generally get louder with inspiration. Left-sided ones are usually louder in expiration.

**Effect of position.** With standing, most murmurs diminish, but the murmur of hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy becomes louder and that of mitral valve prolapse lengthens. With squatting, most murmurs become louder, while those two usually soften.

## Mnemonics

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Bell or diaphragm?** The bell is for the low sounds: S3, S4 and the rumble of mitral stenosis. The diaphragm is for the high sounds: S1, S2, snaps, clicks and most murmurs.
{% /callout %}

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="S1 is caused by closure of the mitral and tricuspid valves at the onset of ventricular systole. S2 is the closure of the semilunar valves." %}
The first heart sound is caused by closure of:
- A. The aortic and pulmonary valves
- B. The pulmonary and tricuspid valves
- C. The mitral and tricuspid valves
- D. The aortic and mitral valves
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="In inspiration the capacitance of the pulmonary vascular bed rises, which delays P2 and so widens the split. Physiological splitting disappears on standing." %}
Normal splitting of the second heart sound is:
- A. Widest in expiration
- B. Widest in inspiration
- C. Fixed throughout the respiratory cycle
- D. Present only when standing
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="A wide, fixed split of S2 is characteristic of atrial septal defect. In RBBB the split is wide but varies with respiration." %}
A wide and fixed split of S2 is characteristic of:
- A. Atrial septal defect
- B. Right bundle branch block
- C. Left bundle branch block
- D. Pulmonary stenosis
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="S3 is low pitched and arises during rapid ventricular filling. It is a normal finding in children and young adults, but in older adults it indicates systolic heart failure." %}
A third heart sound in an elderly patient most likely indicates:
- A. Normal ageing
- B. Mitral stenosis only
- C. Atrial fibrillation
- D. Systolic heart failure
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="S3, S4 and the mid-diastolic murmur of mitral stenosis are low-frequency sounds and are best heard with the bell. The diaphragm is for high-frequency sounds such as S1 and S2." %}
Which of these is best heard with the bell of the stethoscope?
- A. S1
- B. S3
- C. Opening snap
- D. S2
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="Reversed splitting is audible in expiration and not in inspiration, the opposite of normal. It occurs in LBBB, severe aortic stenosis and severe left ventricular dysfunction." %}
Reversed (paradoxical) splitting of S2 is seen in:
- A. Atrial septal defect
- B. Tetralogy of Fallot
- C. Left bundle branch block
- D. Pulmonary hypertension
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="What causes the first and second heart sounds?" %}
S1 is closure of the mitral and tricuspid valves at the start of ventricular systole. S2 is closure of the aortic and pulmonary valves at the end of systole. The sound comes from tensing of the closed valves and vibration of the heart and blood.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Where are S1 and S2 best heard?" %}
S1 is best heard at the apex (mitral area). S2 is best heard in the aortic and pulmonary areas, and its split is best appreciated in the pulmonary area.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Explain the physiological splitting of S2." %}
The aortic valve closes before the pulmonary valve. In inspiration the pulmonary vascular bed expands and delays P2, so the split widens. It narrows in expiration and disappears on standing.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What are the causes of a loud S1 and a soft S1?" %}
Loud: mitral stenosis, short PR interval, tachycardia, hyperkinetic states. Soft: mitral regurgitation, long PR interval, calcified stiff mitral valve, obesity, emphysema, pericardial effusion.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="When is a third heart sound normal?" %}
In children, adolescents, young adults, athletes and pregnancy. In older adults it points to systolic heart failure.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is the fourth heart sound and why is it usually inaudible?" %}
It is produced by atrial contraction pushing blood into the ventricle. It is very weak and low in frequency, so it is normally not heard. An audible S4 is typical of ischaemic heart disease and a stiff ventricle.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Which part of the stethoscope do you use for which sounds?" %}
The bell for low-frequency sounds such as S3, S4 and the mid-diastolic murmur of mitral stenosis. The diaphragm for high-frequency sounds such as S1, S2, opening snap, clicks and systolic murmurs.
{% /viva %}

